// ──────────────────────────── types ──────────────────────────────────────

delegate Task RequestDelegate(Context ctx);

record Problem(int Status, string Title, string? Detail);

class Context(string path)
{
    public string Path { get; } = path;
    public int Status { get; set; } = 200;
    /// <summary>true once the status line and headers have been sent (Response.HasStarted)</summary>
    public bool HasStarted { get; set; }
    public Dictionary<string, string> Headers { get; } = new();
    public Problem? Problem { get; set; }
}

drills.explanation:
The middleware is a try/catch around the rest of the pipeline:

```csharp
return next => async ctx =>
{
    try { await next(ctx); }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        log.Add("unhandled: " + ex.Message);
        if (ctx.HasStarted) throw;          // too late to change status or headers
        ctx.Headers.Clear();
        ctx.Headers["Cache-Control"] = "no-cache,no-store";
        ctx.Headers["Pragma"] = "no-cache";
        ctx.Headers["Expires"] = "-1";
        ctx.Status = 500;
        ctx.Problem = new Problem(500, "An error occurred while processing your request.", null);
    }
};
```

The tests catch two plausible wrong versions: one that ignores `HasStarted` and rewrites a response already on the wire (the boundary test sees a 500 instead of the rethrown `IOException`), and one that copies `ex.Message` into the problem's detail, which leaks secrets such as a connection string to the client. The wrong-order test shows why the template registers the handler first: a middleware outside it is simply not inside the try.

